    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "A shell colon does nothing. Use it anyway",
      "url": "https://refp.se/articles/your-shell-and-the-magic-colon",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/eidh3u",
      "score": 52,
      "comments": 13,
      "author": "refp",
      "published": "2026-07-25T11:33:00Z",
      "bullets": [
        "The POSIX shell colon operator is a built-in no-op returning exit status 0, avoiding subprocess creation overhead.",
        "It safely evaluates parameter expansion side effects like `${VAR:=default}` without executing the expanded string value as a command.",
        "Key use cases include efficient file truncation (`: > file`), lightweight infinite loops (`while :`), and conditional branch placeholders."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/security",
      "category": "security",
      "title": "I Inspected My Take-Home Interview Project. It Was a Whole Operation",
      "url": "https://citizendot.github.io/articles/fake-job-interview-git-hook-malware/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/5nhto6",
      "score": 90,
      "comments": 11,
      "author": "trousers",
      "published": "2026-07-23T01:54:48Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Attacker campaigns use fake technical take-home interviews to deliver malware hidden in project dependencies and malicious build scripts.",
        "Executing untrusted interview code (e.g., via npm install) deploys credential stealers targeting SSH keys, browser databases, and API tokens.",
        "Engineers must isolate interview assignments using sandboxed containers or ephemeral VMs and restrict network access before running local tests."
      ]
    },
